
# Project overview

A web app that generates looping bike routes for cyclists. The user drops a pin on a Leaflet map; a Python backend searches an OSM-derived graph and streams candidate routes; the React UI lets them browse, sort, and filter those routes. It runs locally and is being prepared as a public desktop demo on a small VPS (many visitors, usually one at a time; no accounts: each visitor's routes stay in their browser, the server only keeps shared map tiles and throwaway in-memory state). See `DEPLOY.md` and `deploy/` (Caddy in front: HTTPS, the built UI, `/ws` and `/healthz` to the backend on localhost; the backend as a systemd service; `setup.sh` / `update.sh`).

## Split

- Frontend: Vite + React in `route-loop-finder/`. Talks only over WebSocket, not REST: `/ws` on the page's own origin (`hooks/useWebSocket.js`; `VITE_WS_URL` overrides). The Vite dev and preview servers proxy `/ws` to the backend (port `LOOP_PORT`, default 8765), as the deployed web server does.
- Backend: Python in `backend/`. One process. No global graph: `map_store.py` keeps OSM roads as 0.1° tiles in `backend/map_data/` (downloaded from Overpass once, on demand), `areas.py` builds and caches a search graph per start pin and max distance with `graph_builder.py` (tiles within max/2 + margin; a few seconds), and every generation keeps its own graph. Node ids are OSM node ids. `backend/graphs/*.gpickle` are test data for `tools/` only.

Elevation: a failed SRTM download switches elevation sampling to the graph's node elevations interpolated along each edge for 10 minutes, then SRTM is tried again. The downloaded tiles' roads (`TILE_ROADS`) and `GRAPH_EDGES` of the current area feed the "Blank road network" map style, which the map also falls back to after a few tile errors. See `RUNNING.md` (settings) and `TESTING.md` (the checks).

## UI

The right panel (`ControlPanel.jsx`) always fits the window; only lists scroll, inside their own boxes. With no generation open it shows the plan (`PlanCard.jsx`), the "Your loops" / "Favorites" tabs (`GenerationsList.jsx`, `FavoritesList.jsx`, their own scroll box) and, at the bottom, Advanced settings, which opens as a page over the whole panel (`AdvancedSettings.jsx`; Back or Esc closes it; a generation opening closes it, so going back shows the plan). With a generation open it shows `GenerationPanel.jsx`: header (back, name — click to rename, ⋯ menu: rename, plan again from here, settings used, delete), the through / avoid tools (always visible), Sort (a dropdown: Best = found order, Length, Climb rate, Total climb, Difficulty, Loop share, Turns, Discomfort, Similar; the arrow button reverses and selects the new first route; changing the variable keeps the shown route), filters (Length by default, "+ Filter" adds any other), the route cards in their own scroll box with ‹ › above them (`RouteCard.jsx`: length, climb, sparkline; the mouse wheel scrolls the list one card per notch (a trackpad: one card per card's height of movement; the card it brings under the pointer is previewed); the selected card shows a few more numbers and is kept in the middle of the box, except a clicked card, which stays under the pointer and only scrolls enough to show whole; hovering a card shows that route, with its arrows, in place of the selected one, which drops to a faint line until the pointer leaves the list (the gap between cards keeps the preview; only a real pointer move previews, so a card the list scrolls under a still pointer does not, and a new selection ends the preview); the elevation chart stays on the selected route), and More routes at the bottom (a button, plus a length / roads chip that opens their settings). Every generation has a pin with its route count; generations that start at the same place (within `SAME_START_M`, 20 m; `utils/pathSetGroups.js`) share one pin whose badge counts all their routes, and clicking it opens a menu of them (`MapView/SetPins.jsx`). After Find loops the start pin is cleared and the generation's pin takes its place. "Your loops" rows show the route count, the lengths the routes actually have and the date; a pencil renames, a bin deletes; hovering a row highlights its pin (orange); opening one from the list centres the map on its pin, in the part of the map the panel leaves free. **Favorites** (`hooks/useFavorites.js`, `utils/routeIdentity.js`): S or a star (on each card and beside "Route n of m") favorites a route; "Favorites" in the filter row lists only the open generation's favorites (the shown route stays if it is one; one unfavorited there stays listed until another route is shown). Favorites are drawn in the favorite colour, the theme colour's complement (`--color-favorite`, hue + 180°), when bold or previewed from their card, and their cards are marked; faint, they look like every other route. The plan has two tabs under the plan card: "Your loops" and "Favorites" (`FavoritesList.jsx`, `hooks/useFavoritesView.js`): every favorite, grouped by start (`groupFavorites`), all drawn faint on the map, the start pin and its circle hidden (a click away from favorites does nothing); the tab keeps its own painted roads and areas, which only filter favorites (switching tabs swaps them; Find loops always sends the plan's); its Road tool paints road pieces straight from the downloaded map tiles under the favorites (`GET_ROAD_SEGMENTS {tiles}` → `ROAD_SEGMENTS {tile, lines: [{u, v, name, pts}]}`, `tile_roads.tile_segments`), so no start or area is needed; a row or a click near one on the map selects it (bold, its elevation chart without Reverse, brought into view beside the panel; arrows step, S unfavorites, Esc deselects); a group's "Open" (or its pin) opens the generation in favorites only with that favorite shown; a group whose generations are all deleted offers "Plan here" (the start pin goes there). A route's identity is a key of its exact coordinates (OpenStreetMap node positions, the same in every generation and map build), either way round, so a route found again elsewhere is already a favorite; the centroid is not used (it shifts with the direction, and different routes can share one). Favorites are their own IndexedDB store (`favorites`, a copy of the route with its generation's id, name and start), so they outlive the generation. Map display options are behind the gear icon (`ViewOptions.jsx`), among them "Painted roads & areas": off hides them in pan mode (they still filter; the tools summary says "hidden"; they cannot be double-clicked away), and any active tool shows them again. What the active tool does is shown at the top of the map, not in the panel.

The plan (`PlanCard.jsx`): map status for the start pin, Route length (two-handle slider, mi/km toggle), Roads (Quiet / Balanced / Any), Search area (Automatic or a saved custom boundary; "New boundary…", a pencil to edit), Route through / avoid (the tools), Find loops.

**Custom search boundaries** (`utils/boundary.js`, `hooks/useBoundaries.js`, `ControlPanel/BoundaryEditor.jsx`, `MapView/BoundaryLayers.jsx`, backend `boundary.py`): a boundary is `{ id, name, shapes }`, its shapes (circle / box / freeform, the painted areas' form) together one area. The editor is a page over the panel: add shapes (C / B / F, drag on the map; a freehand outline is simplified to at most 40 points, none closer than a tenth of its size), adjust them with handles (a circle's centre and edge, a box's corners and middle, a freeform shape's points, small handles between them add one, double-click removes one, a middle handle moves it), remove them from the list; Enter (Use boundary) saves it (localStorage `boundaries`) and the plan uses it (`genSettings.boundary`, a copy), Esc cancels. The server keeps only the roads inside it (`graph_builder.build_graph(boundary=)`, one vectorized point test; the trim by road distance still drops what no route can reach) and downloads only the tiles it overlaps within reach (`areas.tiles_needed`); areas are cached per boundary. A pin outside starts on the nearest road inside. A generation keeps its boundary in its settings (shown grey and dotted; the "Search boundary" display option); adding to it with another boundary gives it that one (`CONTINUE_GENERATION` settings: a new boundary rebuilds the session's area). The start pin is set by clicking the map (pan mode), dragging the pin, or the search box (`SearchBox.jsx`, top left, opens on hover or `/`; Photon via `utils/geocode.js`, `VITE_GEOCODER_URL` overrides). Units (`utils/units.js`, React context) only change display: settings, messages, and route properties stay in miles and feet. Generations are named after the place only ("Dilworth, Charlotte"; a second one from there gets " 2"). When generations already start where the pin is, the plan shows "Loops from here": Find loops becomes Add loops and adds to the newest of them (or the one chosen; "New set" makes another). Adding opens that generation, gives it the plan's settings (length, roads, Advanced) and painted roads and areas, and sends `CONTINUE_GENERATION` with the first-batch size: the server starts a fresh search seeded with every route the generation has, so nothing repeats. "Plan again from here" (the ⋯ menu) goes back to the plan at the generation's start with it chosen.

## Map gestures

One pointer reach for everything: 24 px (at least 30 m), `pointerReachM` in `utils/routeHover.js`. Hover work goes through `utils/mapHover.js` (`attachProximityHover`: whole-map mousemove, one evaluation per animation frame, callbacks only when the result changes, layers moved in place), shared by the route hover marker (`MapView/RouteHover.jsx`) and the Road tool (`MapView/RoadTool.jsx`). The hovered point (`hooks/useHoverPoint.js`, outside React state) is shared by the map marker and the elevation chart, so pointer moves re-render only those two. Pan mode (`MapView/PanInteractions.jsx`): in the route view a click near routes shows the one there (clicking again where several run together steps through them, quick clicks too; nothing is filtered or sent); in the plan a click moves the start. Only a click pressed in pan mode counts (releasing a drawn area switches back to pan; that release does not move the start). Double-click a painted road (a Ctrl-click road goes whole), an area's dotted outline, or an old pin removes it; double-click never zooms. Routes and selections on the map take no pointer events themselves.

## WebSocket contract

Messages are JSON with a `type` field. The server trusts nothing in them (`backend/limits.py`): numbers are clamped to what the UI offers (length to `LOOP_MAX_MILES`, 50; `num_paths` ≤ 200; loop share 0.2–0.9; same-route distance 10–1000 m), bad coordinates and ids drop the message, selections and a rebuild's centroids are capped, messages are at most 4 MB and rate-limited per connection, connections are capped per visitor address (behind the local web server: the last `X-Forwarded-For`), and `LOOP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` restricts which sites' pages may connect. `GET /healthz` on the same port answers `ok`. On connect the server sends `ALGORITHMS_LIST` and `MAP_INFO {tiles, mb, tileDeg, coverage: [[row, col], ...]}` (the downloaded tiles; sent again after an area is built, as tiles may have been downloaded). `GET_TILE_ROADS {tiles: [[row, col], ...]}` → one `TILE_ROADS {tile, lines: [[class, lat, lng, lat, lng, ...], ...], missing?}` per tile (`tile_roads.py`: every way of the tile simplified to ~4 m; class 0 major, 1 minor, 2 local, 3 path): the offline basemap (`MapView/OfflineBasemap.jsx`, `utils/mapTiles.js`) draws them for the downloaded tiles in view, greys out the rest, offers "Go to the nearest" downloaded area, and warns when a start needs tiles that are not downloaded.

Map: placing or moving the start pin (or changing max distance or boundary) sends `PREFETCH_AREA {lat, lng, max_path_len, boundary, token}` (debounced; `boundary` `{shapes}` or null, `token` the UI's id of the request, echoed in its `MAP_PROGRESS` / `AREA_READY` / `MAP_ERROR` so an answer for an earlier max or boundary of the same pin is ignored); the server cancels that connection's queued downloads for the old pin, downloads missing tiles with `MAP_PROGRESS {scope: 'prefetch', lat, lng, phase: 'download'|'queue'|'build', done, total, mb}` (about once a second while downloading; `done`/`total` are tiles, `mb` what the running request received), builds the area, and answers `AREA_READY {scope: 'prefetch', areaId, lat, lng (the pin asked about), maxMiles, start (where routes begin), nodes, edges}` or `MAP_ERROR {scope, lat, lng, error}`. The client ignores messages for a pin it no longer has.

**Taking turns** (`backend/work_queue.py`): searches (a batch of routes) and area builds of every visitor run one at a time (`LOOP_SEARCH_SLOTS`, 1), ordered by when the visitor's request arrived (a context variable set per message), so each visitor is served start to finish in turn; map downloads do not take a turn. A waiting request gets `MAP_PROGRESS {phase: 'queue', ahead}` (for its `pathSetId`, or `scope: 'prefetch'` for the pin) whenever the number ahead changes, and `MAP_PROGRESS {phase: 'search'}` (no text) when a search's turn comes; the UI shows "Waiting for another search to finish…" (`utils/mapProgress.js`) in the plan's map status, the route list and the busy line. Stop, a new search, or closing the tab leaves the line.

**Memory** (for a 4 GB server): at most `LOOP_MAX_AREAS` (4) built areas and `LOOP_MAX_SESSIONS` (12) generations, `LOOP_TILE_CACHE` (16) parsed tiles. Every minute a sweep drops generations and areas unused for `LOOP_IDLE_MINUTES` (30), and generations whose area left the cache (each would keep a whole graph alive); with no area left it empties the tile cache and hands memory back (`malloc_trim`). Dropped generations are rebuilt from the browser's copy when used again.

`GET_GRAPH_EDGES {areaId}` → `GRAPH_EDGES {areaId, lines: [{u, v, name, pts}]}` (u, v are OSM node ids).

`START_GENERATION` carries `lat, lng, min_path_len, max_path_len` (miles), `boundary` (a custom search boundary, or null), `road_preference` (`quiet` | `balanced` | `any`; `road_weights` instead when the Advanced table is custom), `rural_scale`, `loop_ratio`, `num_paths`, tuning weights, `selections`.

Generation is a stream on that same socket: `START_GENERATION` → `PATHSET_CREATED` → (`MAP_PROGRESS {pathSetId, ...}` while its map downloads or builds, usually none after a prefetch) → `AREA_READY {pathSetId, areaId, ...}` → many `PATH_RECEIVED` (GeoJSON Feature + properties) → `GENERATION_COMPLETE`. If the map cannot be downloaded or has no usable roads, `GENERATION_COMPLETE` comes with `added: 0`, `resumable: false` and the reason in `note`.

A generated "loop" is a **lollipop**: out-and-back stem + a loop in the middle + the stem reversed. That is intended, not a bug.

The start is where the pin joins the roads: the OSM node nearest the pin on the nearest road that leads to the part of the network loops can use (`graph_builder._snap_node`; a path that no rideable road joins is skipped), also in the middle of a block or at the end of a dead-end street. The road is cut there, the shortest way from it to the rest of the network is kept through the dead-end pruning (a dead end becomes the stem of every route), and chains are never merged through it. The plan's start pin then moves onto that start (prefetch `AREA_READY` carries `start`; the area cache also matches a pin on an area's start, so asking again from there reuses it). Only when a loop could not come back to it (the end of a one-way road) does the start fall back to the node nearest the pin of the largest part of the network that can be ridden both ways.

Route exploration is client-side. Areas and painted roads filter generated routes by elevation-profile coordinates (about every 50 m). Nothing about these filters goes to the backend except as constraints (below). The Similar sort walks routes that share ground, starting at the shortest or longest match.

Generation runs in a worker thread as a background task per connection; `STOP_GENERATION` sets a stop flag the search loop checks every iteration, and the server then sends `GENERATION_COMPLETE` with `stopped: true` (paths already sent are kept). A new `START_GENERATION` stops the previous one first. Generations are saved in the browser (IndexedDB, `utils/generationsDb.js`, via `usePathSets`; favorite routes in a second store): routes, settings, name, painted roads and areas, written a moment after each change and when the page is hidden; loaded at start; kept until deleted (with undo). One generation is open at a time (display mode); going back shows the Plan card and "Your loops".

**Constraints** are the one exception to "filters never reach the backend". Selections are areas (`circle` / `box` / `freeform`, include only) and painted roads (`edges`: `[u, v]` node pairs with `kind` edge / road / area); old generations may still hold pins (`point`), which are shown and can be removed but no longer made. The tools bar (`ToolsBar.jsx`: Pan K, Road R, Circle C, Box B, Freeform F, Through | Avoid switch D — Shift flips it for one gesture, Undo Z) is always visible, in the plan and the route view. Road tool (`utils/paintRoads.js`): click paints one edge, Ctrl-click the connected run of same-named edges; green = required (bright for one edge, dark for a road group, which means "any one of its edges"), red = excluded; a click on a painted road clears it in either mode (a Ctrl-click clears the road if all of it is painted, else paints it whole). An include area is a green dotted outline. An excluded area is a red dotted outline; it is stored as one `edges` selection (`kind: 'area'`) with its red roads and its `outline`, so the server and the filters see plain red roads. Its roads are drawn only while the Road tool points inside it; a click there turns one back on (grey dashed) or off again. Roads already painted when an area is drawn keep their colour. A red road inside a green area is allowed (routes must enter the area on one of its other roads; the server drops excluded roads from an area's required set). All of it is a client-side filter *and* a search constraint: `START_GENERATION` carries `selections` (an excluded area with every road turned back on is not sent), so the initial search honours them, and they stay with the generation (shown again when it is reopened).

The area's roads come from `GET_GRAPH_EDGES {areaId}` → `GRAPH_EDGES {areaId, lines: [{u, v, name, pts}]}` (the open generation's area, else the start pin's; fetched once per area and kept for the last few; `utils/graphRoads.js` indexes them for nearest-edge, road groups, and edges-in-shape; drawn faintly over tiles, "Show Road Network", and as the blank-road-network basemap). Input mode also draws a dashed circle of radius max/2 around the start pin: no route can leave it.

Every generation is **continuable**: the server keeps a session per `pathSetId` in one process-wide LRU (`SESSIONS`, `LOOP_MAX_SESSIONS`, 12; not tied to the connection, so a reload resumes it) with the start, settings, area, every sent route's centroid, and a parked `tree_guided` generator. A lost session (restart, eviction) is rebuilt from the `rebuild` payload `{lat, lng, settings, centroids, batches}` the client sends with `OPEN_GENERATION` (opening a generation whose area this server run has not sent: answers `AREA_READY {pathSetId}`) and `CONTINUE_GENERATION`. In display mode, More routes (button or M) and adding an include (green road, area) send `CONTINUE_GENERATION {pathSetId, selections, num_paths, algorithm, settings, rebuild}` with *every* current selection; `settings` carry the More routes length and roads: if they differ from the session's, the parked generator is replaced by a fresh one seeded with every known centroid (and the area grows when the max does, with `AREA_READY {pathSetId}`). The server answers `CONSTRAINTS_RESOLVED` (snapped pins; selections that hit no road are removed with a notice), `GENERATION_RESUMED`, `PATH_RECEIVED`… (with `properties.batch` / `via`), `GENERATION_COMPLETE {added, note}`. New routes cross every include (stem counts), avoid every red road, and never repeat a route already in the generation (the parked generator resumes in place; any other algorithm runs fresh, seeded with the generation's centroids). Painting red (roads or an area) only filters; More routes (M) sends the current set any time; `num_paths` is `routes_per_pin` (default 10). Removing a constraint (double-click, unpaint, Z, Clear) stops an in-flight batch without restarting one. `GENERATION_ERROR {code: 'SESSION_EXPIRED'}` only comes when there is no session and no `rebuild`.

## Checks

`npm test` in `route-loop-finder/` (Vitest: `src/**/*.test.js`, the pure logic in `utils/`), `python -m unittest discover -s tests` in `backend/` (the graph builder), and `python tools/check_frontend.py` in `backend/` (the real app in headless Chromium, on the Boone and Charlotte tiles, no map downloads). See `TESTING.md`.
